The last 11 steps, the proof is complete! Starting from the assumption that the perpendicular lines are drawn on each of the three sides at the intersection point I of the bisectors of the two interior angles of the triangle, which was the starting condition, through the three green perpendicular lines (radius of the inscribed circle) drawn perpendicular to the three sides of the triangle at the intersection point I and the auxiliary line connecting the third vertex, the conclusion is that the bisectors of the three interior angles meet at one point (incenter I), and that the vertical distances from the incenter to the three sides are all the same (radius of the inscribed circle). It has been clearly confirmed that this is true. The key point to remember here is that the circumcenter is the same distance to the 'three vertices' (OA=OB=OC), and the incenter is the same distance to the 'foot of the perpendicular line on the three sides' (ID=IE=IF=r). It is also directly connected to the formula for the area of ​​the triangle: S = 1/2·r·(a+b+c). Finally, ask a deeper question. If you draw the bisector of one interior angle of a triangle and the bisector of two exterior angles instead of the interior angles, what kind of circle (excircle) will be created outside the triangle? Experiment by changing the values ​​and shapes directly on the math homepage in the link in the description!
